#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097522} :activity/id #uuid "b8439109-03d0-56d9-8a44-f5a5b744e4b7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048742 :topic/id #uuid "ba72c50b-60dc-50dc-ae9b-3cc018d38d60" :topic/math-academy-id 1049} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097843 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of linear equations and its respective augmented matrix $M\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/7c/7c2283c7de572acfc58b443499598554ecf3d26b1a13c48c80b8d6aadf7b576a.svg)\n\nThe matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form, but we can transform it even further into the **reduced row echelon form** (RREF) using the standard **Gauss-Jordan** method. \n\nThe algorithm is similar to the algorithm of converting a matrix to row echelon form, except that we need to make *all* the pivot entries equal to $1$ and *all* the entries below *and above* a pivot (in a pivot column) equal to $0.$\n\nIn our case, we convert $M$ to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final matrix is in reduced row echelon form:\n\n- all the pivot entries are equal to $1$ (here we have $\\color{red}1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- all the entries below and above a pivot are equal to ${\\color{blue}0}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ce056e74-9707-52d3-b172-483ca14e1f8f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3160 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "78885948-fa6f-5f46-8f96-e5da861960dd" :step/math-academy-id 17209 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097844 :tutorial/content "For a $3\\times 3$ system with $3$ pivots, the Gauss-Jordan method can be described as follows:\n\n- First, reduce the matrix to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination method (also making all pivot entries equal to $1$). \n![](images/5c/5c0f5ac3d95843b142c90e8ce83378ea2b2ad693baba6670e0cde46a449f5225.svg)\n\n- We use the third row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{33}$ equal to $0.$ Namely, \n\n- if $a_{23} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-a_{23})R_3$, and\n\n- if $a_{13} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{13})R_3$.\n\n- We use the second row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{22}$ equal to $0$. Namely, \n\n- if $a_{12} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{12})R_2$.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to reduced row echelon form.\n\n![](images/9c/9c817628a3c646a928f12548a109b8136bb0a5977fab6e4d18e972690d81159b.svg)\n\n**Note:** Unlike the row echelon form, the *reduced* row echelon form of a matrix is unique." :tutorial/id #uuid "f74e6075-9c2a-5495-ab21-470711559f2a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3161 :tutorial/title "General Algorithm (RREF)"} :step/id #uuid "278dc07d-f5da-5098-a755-d5a72662e2ac" :step/math-academy-id 17210 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097524}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096674 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096461 :question/id #uuid "de738faf-e020-5058-b6a5-48020212fbc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4709"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7294399b-b139-5ee6-824c-5e28e4fc49ff" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416435}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a 3x3 System That Is Already in Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "7f602ae0-3af8-588f-bdb5-c0995e19908f" :step/math-academy-id 17211 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096675 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096462 :question/id #uuid "239a008b-4c67-5334-8285-3c46b773fead" :question/math-academy-id "e-4710"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95a32907-8304-5dfd-8759-9e91503445bf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393410}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a 3x3 System That Is Not in Row Echelon Form"} :step/id #uuid "c80be1fb-c96f-5733-8d79-5c777f840ef9" :step/math-academy-id 17212 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096546 :question/id #uuid "283e9a4a-443f-579f-b272-248446d3a49f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b010a518-7aa3-5b53-9a6d-ef0779ff0dfa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416437}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With Two Pivots"} :step/id #uuid "f3553e51-447b-5d4a-b387-1bc43d2a825d" :step/math-academy-id 23346 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096746 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096547 :question/id #uuid "8de46974-719c-5ac2-a43c-83d1c67c57b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6234"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa91091b-5368-5d80-b87d-08b3524177f3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416438}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a Singular 3x3 System With One Pivot"} :step/id #uuid "4208bcd7-ee35-5977-8afd-7beb404e8b75" :step/math-academy-id 23347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097869 :tutorial/content "When a $3\\times3$ system of equations has two pivot columns instead of three, we can still reduce its augmented matrix to reduced row echelon form. The process is similar to what we did before. \n\n- First, reduce the matrix to row echelon form using the standard Gaussian elimination method (also making all pivot entries equal to $1$). \n ![](images/99/992cfaabdb3490e99553fbbdfee0089b399306e3e65720fc6a70014bfa35321a.svg)\n\n- We use the second row and necessary row operations to make the entries above $a_{22}$ equal to $0$. Namely, \n\n- if $a_{12} \\neq 0$, then we use the row operation $R_1:=R_1 + (-a_{12})R_2$.\n\nCarrying out the above algorithm will convert the matrix to reduced row echelon form.\n\n![](images/3d/3da5751a339ff2ee85d424b42a72f5270f1be076407a33abd1b79e0f082639d5.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "baed23fb-560e-56ad-aea3-46f3728ebfff" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3949 :tutorial/title "Using the Gauss-Jordan Method on a 3x3 System With Two Pivots"} :step/id #uuid "e4f6891c-3e25-562a-900b-9617b1000f05" :step/math-academy-id 23416 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097634}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097870 :tutorial/content "It's also possible to write the augmented matrix for a $3\\times 3$ system of linear equations with only one pivot in reduced row echelon form. \n\nLet's see an example of how this works." :tutorial/id #uuid "e3748ee3-9d5d-5a6a-808f-ef8f99cbd1aa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3950 :tutorial/title "Using the Gauss-Jordan Method on a 3x3 System With One Pivot"} :step/id #uuid "541dad41-3dc8-5018-acca-4d79ebbbda41" :step/math-academy-id 23417 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097635}}] :activity/title "Reduced Row Echelon Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096857}]}
